    "turn_text": "Hi everyone, I am really honored that Hilary Scoulton took some time in the midst of campaigning for Michigan's third district congressional seat to talk with me about refugia. Hilary's broad perspective ranges all the way from inside the August halls of the U.S. Department of Justice to Michigan's farms and fields where Hilary provided legal representation for migrant workers. Here's what I wanted to know. When it comes to creating refugia, especially for vulnerable people, does government policy have a role? Or is this all about changing hearts and minds on a community level? Hillary helps us think through those questions, as Christians and as citizens. This is the last regular episode of the Refugia podcast. I hope you've had a chance to listen through the other episodes and look for our final summary episode too. And of course, as always, thanks for listening. Today I'm talking with Hillary Scoulton. Hillary is an attorney who worked for the Department of Justice under the Obama administration and then moved back to Grand Rapids, Michigan, her hometown to serve with the Michigan Immigrant Rights Center. These days, she is running for Congress. She is a Democratic candidate from Michigan's Third District. Hillary is here to talk with me today about how policies and systems can prevent or encourage the kinds of refugia that help all people thrive. Hey, Hillary.",
